---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import StageStrip from "../components/StageStrip.astro";
import RepCard from "../components/RepCard.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
	title="Lead From The Hip — leadership development that measures behavior"
	description="Assessment-led leadership coaching that turns your dispositions into specific routines, then measures whether they stuck. For anyone who leads a team."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Leader development · assessment · performance coaching"
		standfirst="Knowledge is cheap. Behavior is everything. Success requires getting in more reps at doing the right things until they become habit. That's the game: Pick the two or three behaviors that actually cost you, then practice them relentlessly. Not until you get them right — until you can't get them wrong."
		primaryCta={{ label: "See how it works →", href: "#method" }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: "Work with me", href: "/work-with-me" }}
	>
		Less knowing,<br />
		<span class="accent">more doing.</span>
	</Hero>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="01"
			title="Why studying it never made it stick"
			lede="You have probably already done at least one of these. All three are study. None of them is practice, and none of them ever put you under real resistance."
		/>
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The report that named you accurately.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					It was well-built and it was right about you. You nodded. It stung a little.
					Then it went in a drawer, because a description of who you are is not an
					instruction for what to do on Thursday.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The book with the secret sauce.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					Five pillars. Ten habits. One framework, guaranteed to work for every leader
					who reads it — as though a weekly one-to-one costs a naturally organized
					person and a naturally spontaneous one the same amount. It doesn't. There is
					no secret sauce, and pretending otherwise is why the resolution lasted nine
					days.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The feedback that arrived once a year.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					Aggregated, anonymized, delivered in a form your company chose, about a
					period you can no longer remember. You didn't pick the raters. You never saw
					whether anything you changed afterward registered with anyone.
				</p>
			</Card>
		</div>
		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-xl">
			Every one of those was study. Necessary, but never enough on its own — what was
			missing was practice, and hard training: practice against real resistance, not just
			repetition when it's easy.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="method" class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="02"
			title="The method"
			lede="Three stages, borrowed from Musonius Rufus. Applied to the habits you already run on, how you act with the people around you, and the judgment that tells you whether either one is working."
		/>
		<StageStrip />
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="03"
			title="What a rep actually looks like"
			lede="Not a principle. Not a mindset. A specific thing you do, cued by a specific moment, that takes under a minute and requires no change to your character. This is what gets rehearsed in practice, then run for real in hard training."
		/>
		<RepCard />
		<p class="text-muted text-sm mt-4 measure">
			Thirty seconds of process. No new personality required. That is the entire design
			principle — the rep has to be cheap enough that you still run it on the worst week
			of the quarter.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader number="04" title="Who this is for" lede="Two honest lists." />
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This works if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
					<li>Have tried to change a habit before and watched it not stick</li>
					<li>Care about how you come across to people, not just what you meant to do</li>
					<li>
						Are willing to practice something small and specific, on purpose, even
						when it's inconvenient
					</li>
					<li>Would rather find out whether it worked than feel good about having tried</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This is not for you if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
					<li>Want a label more than a behavior to change</li>
					<li>Are looking for a diagnosis of someone else, not yourself</li>
					<li>Want a program to roll out to a team or an organization</li>
					<li>
						Need clinical support — that is a different profession and I will say so
						and refer
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="05"
			title="Where the study happens"
			lede="A pattern, a belief, one specific thing to try — that's a post, most weeks, on the Substack. Reading it isn't the work. Running it is."
		/>
		<a
			href="https://substack.com/@leadfromthehip"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Read the Substack →
		</a>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="06"
			title="If you want more"
			lede="Most of what's here is free — the method, the examples, the Substack. From time to time I take on a handful of clients who want the full version: an assessment, a written plan, and the ninety-day proof that it worked."
		/>
		<a
			href="/work-with-me"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Work with me →
		</a>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
